  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Exponential Envelopes",
    "lesson_core_move": "Identify the exponential envelope $e^{\\sigma t}$ that bounds the oscillatory components of $e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}$ and use the sign of $\\sigma$ to interpret damping, growth, or constant-amplitude behavior.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Envelopes",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Exponential Envelopes",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "An exponential envelope is the real exponential factor $e^{\\sigma t}$ inside $e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}$. It controls the size of the oscillation, while $e^{j\\omega t}$ controls the oscillation itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}=e^{\\sigma t}e^{j\\omega t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the signal as $e^{\\sigma t}e^{j\\omega t}$. That split separates the amplitude envelope from the oscillatory factor you already know.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left|e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\right|=e^{\\sigma t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The envelope is the magnitude scale: $\\left|e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\right|=e^{\\sigma t}$. If you look at the real part, the oscillation stays between $-e^{\\sigma t}$ and $e^{\\sigma t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\Re\\{e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}\\}=e^{\\sigma t}\\cos(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The boundary case is $\\sigma=0$, so $e^{\\sigma t}=1$. In that case the envelope is flat and the signal keeps constant amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\sigma=0\\;\\Rightarrow\\;e^{\\sigma t}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the sign of $\\sigma$, you will later read decay when $\\sigma<0$ and growth when $\\sigma>0$, using the same factorization each time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Envelopes",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Envelope in $e^{(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{(-3+j4)t}$, identify the exponential envelope and the oscillatory factor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the mixed exponent $(-3+j4)t$. The real part $-3$ sets the amplitude envelope, and the imaginary part $j4$ gives the oscillation you already know.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{(-3+j4)t}=e^{-3t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the signal as $e^{-3t}e^{j4t}$. That split isolates $e^{-3t}$ as the exponential envelope and leaves $e^{j4t}$ as the oscillatory factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the envelope is $e^{-3t}$. That is the factor that later tells you whether the signal decays or grows from the sign of $\\sigma$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Envelopes",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting a Decaying Envelope with $\\sigma<0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{(-3+j4)t}$, explain how the envelope behaves as $t$ increases.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the negative real part $\\sigma=-3$. That means the same factorization from the previous example will produce an envelope that shrinks as time increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{-3t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write $x(t)=e^{-3t}e^{j4t}$. The factor $e^{-3t}$ is the exponential envelope, and the negative exponent means that envelope decays toward zero as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}e^{-3t}=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the oscillation keeps going, but it is bounded by a shrinking envelope. That is the damping behavior you read from $\\sigma<0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Envelopes",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting a Growing Envelope with $\\sigma>0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{(3+j4)t}$, explain how the envelope behaves as $t$ increases.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the positive real part $\\sigma=3$. That means the same factorization from the previous example will produce an envelope that expands as time increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{3t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write $x(t)=e^{3t}e^{j4t}$. The factor $e^{3t}$ is the exponential envelope, and the positive exponent means that envelope grows without bound as $t$ increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}e^{3t}=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the oscillation keeps going, but it is bounded by an expanding envelope. That is the growth behavior you read from $\\sigma>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Exponential Envelopes",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing a Constant-Amplitude Envelope with $\\sigma=0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{j4t}$, identify the envelope and explain why the amplitude stays constant.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the zero real part: this is the boundary case between decay and growth, so the envelope should be flat.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{(0+j4)t}=e^{0t}e^{j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the signal as $e^{0t}e^{j4t}$. The factor $e^{0t}$ is the envelope, and because $e^{0t}=1$, the amplitude stays constant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{0t}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal only oscillates; it neither grows nor decays. That is the constant-amplitude behavior you read from $\\sigma=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
